include includes/folio-shared.pug
include includes/assignment-demo.pug

html(lang="en")
    head
        +folioHead('Teachers could see how many finished, not who hadn’t | Aaron Benjamin', 'I redesigned the Renaissance Intelligence assignments page so K-12 teachers could see which students hadn’t started, not only how many had finished.', '/classroom-assignment-management.html')
    body.folio.folio--case
        +folioShell

        +folioHeader('work', 'Classroom Assignment Management')

        main#main.folio-main.folio-main--case
            article.folio-case
                header.folio-case__hero
                    figure.folio-case__hero-media
                        img(src="assets/img/folio/project--assignment-management/assignment-management--hero.png", alt="Renaissance Intelligence assignment view with new, overdue, due soon, in-progress, and done groups, and The Long Winter listed as overdue" width="1920" height="1436" fetchpriority="high")
                    .folio-case__intro
                        p.folio-eyebrow Case study
                        h1 Teachers could see how many finished, not who hadn’t
                        p.folio-case__dek My team and I set out to improve a table. What teachers needed was a student name the API didn’t send.
                        p.folio-case__context
                            | More than 40% of U.S. schools rely on&#32;
                            a(href="https://www.renaissance.com/about-us/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer") Renaissance
                            |  for learning data and insights.
                    +caseFacts
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Role
                            dd Senior UX Designer
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Team
                            dd Product manager, product owner, three engineers, and UX research partners
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Timeline
                            dd Four weeks of research and design, followed by two weeks of development
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Outcome
                            dd Met the initiative’s hub-adoption goal. Teachers stayed on the Assignments page instead of opening each product’s report.
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt What I owned
                            dd I co-authored the requirements with the product manager and owned the information architecture, interaction patterns, page content, states, prototypes, handoff, and launch planning.

                +caseSection({labelledby: "belief-title"})
                    h2#belief-title We assumed a better table would be enough
                    p Version 1 of the Assignments page pulled work from several Renaissance products into one table. The team, me included, believed that a table with the right data would give teachers the most flexibility. If they could sort and filter, they could find anything.
                    p Our UX research partners ran moderated sessions with five current Renaissance users and five teachers new to the product suite. Participants reviewed the existing page and two prototypes, looked for specific assignments, and talked through how they would use the page in class.
                    p The flexibility was the problem. Teachers had to sort and filter before they could see who needed help. They ranked urgency by due date, and the table showed when work was assigned. It also showed “11/16” next to Fractions Practice and had no way to say which five.

                +caseSection({labelledby: "turn-title", tint: true})
                    h2#turn-title The API knew how many students finished, not who
                    p Here is the situation we kept coming back to. It is an illustration, not one observed session: a teacher sees that eight of ten students finished an assignment and still can’t see which two didn’t. To find them, the teacher leaves the hub and opens that product’s own report.
                    p No layout could fix that. The initial API returned aggregate completion counts only, so there were no names to show. With engineering and the API owners, I got student IDs added to the payload, along with completion time. With IDs, the page could show student names.
                    p That changed the design question. It stopped being how to display counts and became which assignments and students a teacher should see first.

                +caseSection({labelledby: "decision-title", prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        h2#decision-title I replaced the table with a priority view
                        p Once the names existed, I stopped trying to make the table smarter. The final design puts new assignments first, then groups the rest by overdue, due soon, in progress, and done. Teachers can expand or collapse any group, so the flexibility is still there. It just isn’t the first job.
                        p All ten teachers in the study, five current users and five new to the suite, said the grouping matched how they expected to review assignments.
                    +annotatedFrame({labelledby: "frame-caption", captionId: "frame-caption", images: [{src: "assets/img/folio/project--assignment-management/assignment-management--current-page.png", alt: "Version 1 of the Assignments and Assessments page: seven filter menus above a table of assignments with assigned dates and completion counts", width: 1760, height: 1210}, {src: "assets/img/folio/project--assignment-management/assignment-management--hero.png", alt: "The grouped view: new assignments, then overdue with The Long Winter, then due soon, in progress, and done", width: 1920, height: 1436}], callouts: ["Version 1: seven menus sit above the table. Teachers filtered before they could act.", "Version 1: a count like 11/16 said how many students finished. It couldn’t say who.", "Version 1 showed when work was assigned. The grouped view leads with when it’s due, which is how teachers ranked urgency.", "Grouped view: overdue work sits near the top, so The Long Winter shows up without a search.", "Grouped view: done stays collapsed, so finished work doesn’t push active work down the page."], caption: "Version 1 asked teachers to build their own priority list. The grouped view starts with one. The screenshot’s Done group is longer than the demo below, which shows three finished assignments so the list stays short."})

                +pullQuote('“OMG, this is amazing.”', 'A study participant, after comparing the grouped design with Version 1')

                +caseSection({labelledby: "final-design-title", final: true, prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        h2#final-design-title Student names sit one click from each assignment
                        p Teachers scan by urgency and open an assignment without leaving the page. The Long Winter is open below, sorted so students who haven’t started come first.
                    .folio-demo(aria-label="Interactive reconstruction of the final assignments view")
                        p.folio-demo__note Interactive reconstruction of the final design. Student names and results are illustrative.
                        p.folio-demo__note Open an overdue assignment to see who hasn’t started.
                        .folio-demo__canvas
                            details.folio-demo__group(open)
                                summary.folio-demo__group-summary
                                    img.folio-demo__angle(src="assets/img/folio/project--assignment-management/demo-icons/angle-down.svg", alt="")
                                    span New assignments (1)
                                .folio-demo__group-content
                                    +assignment('Skill Practice: Symmetry & Transformations', '3 complete, 17 in progress, 8 not started', '9/27', 'lightbulb', false, false)

                            details.folio-demo__group(open)
                                summary.folio-demo__group-summary
                                    img.folio-demo__angle(src="assets/img/folio/project--assignment-management/demo-icons/angle-down.svg", alt="")
                                    span Overdue (2)
                                .folio-demo__group-content.folio-demo__group-content--assignments
                                    +assignment('Reading Activity: The Long Winter, Chapter 4', '14 complete, 3 in progress, 11 not started', '9/17', 'book-spine', false, true)
                                    +assignment('Math Challenge: Area & Perimeter', '9 complete, 6 in progress, 13 not started', '9/19', 'calculator', false, false)

                            details.folio-demo__group
                                summary.folio-demo__group-summary
                                    img.folio-demo__angle(src="assets/img/folio/project--assignment-management/demo-icons/angle-down.svg", alt="")
                                    span Due soon (3)
                                .folio-demo__group-content.folio-demo__group-content--assignments
                                    +assignment('Skill Practice: Equivalent Fractions', '12 complete, 10 in progress, 4 not started', '9/22', 'lightbulb', false, false)
                                    +assignment('Reading Activity: The One and Only Ivan, Chapter 6', '15 complete, 7 in progress, 4 not started', '9/23', 'book-spine', false, false)
                                    +assignment('Math Challenge: Ratios & Proportions', '8 complete, 11 in progress, 7 not started', '9/24', 'calculator', false, false)
                            details.folio-demo__group
                                summary.folio-demo__group-summary
                                    img.folio-demo__angle(src="assets/img/folio/project--assignment-management/demo-icons/angle-down.svg", alt="")
                                    span In progress (5)
                                .folio-demo__group-content.folio-demo__group-content--assignments
                                    +assignment('Skill Practice: Adding Integers', '18 complete, 6 in progress, 2 not started', '9/15', 'lightbulb', false, false)
                                    +assignment('Math Challenge: Algebraic Expressions', '13 complete, 9 in progress, 4 not started', '9/16', 'calculator', false, false)
                                    +assignment('Skill Practice: Word Relationships', '16 complete, 8 in progress, 2 not started', '9/16', 'lightbulb', false, false)
                                    +assignment('Reading Activity: Bud, Not Buddy, Chapter 8', '14 complete, 10 in progress, 2 not started', '9/17', 'book-spine', false, false)
                                    +assignment('Math Challenge: Decimal Operations', '11 complete, 12 in progress, 3 not started', '9/18', 'calculator', false, false)
                            details.folio-demo__group
                                summary.folio-demo__group-summary
                                    img.folio-demo__angle(src="assets/img/folio/project--assignment-management/demo-icons/angle-down.svg", alt="")
                                    span Done (3)
                                .folio-demo__group-content.folio-demo__group-content--assignments
                                    +assignment('Skill Practice: Vocabulary Review', '25 complete, 1 in progress, 0 not started', '9/16', 'lightbulb', false, false)
                                    +assignment('Math Challenge: Volume', '26 complete, 0 in progress, 0 not started', '9/15', 'calculator', false, false)
                                    +assignment('Reading Activity: The Giver, Chapter 1', '24 complete, 2 in progress, 0 not started', '9/14', 'book-spine', false, false)

                +caseSection({labelledby: "buildable-title", tint: true, figure: {src: "assets/img/folio/project--assignment-management/assignment-management--object-model.png", alt: "Assignment object model showing shared assignment attributes alongside attributes specific to live lessons and reading activities", caption: "The object model separated the attributes every assignment shared from product-specific detail.", loading: "lazy"}})
                    h2#buildable-title Making it buildable inside the product we had
                    p Each practice and assessment product stored assignments its own way. I facilitated an object-modeling workshop with product and engineering to define one shared assignment object. Every assignment has a title, type, assigned date, and due date. Product-specific detail, such as questions answered or pages read, sits alongside. The interface shows the shared fields first and keeps product context in labels and icons.
                    p The page ran as a React app inside an iframe in an Angular shell, which limited layout, filters, dialogs, and other interactions. Along with the student IDs, we requested timeframe pre-filtering from the API so loading student-level data wouldn’t slow the page.
                    p I ran the working sessions with Design Dash, the object-modeling and prototyping toolset I built. AI-assisted prototypes with realistic mock data let product, research, and engineering compare options in the room and leave with decisions and open questions written down.
                    p We deferred a fuller student-performance summary. Teachers wanted it, but it would have delayed the core fix: showing which assignments and students needed attention.

                +caseOutcome("outcome-title", "Teachers stayed on one page instead of opening each product’s report")
                    p Version 2 launched after four weeks of research and design and two weeks of development. It met the initiative’s goal of moving teachers from individual products into Renaissance Intelligence. Product behavior data showed fewer trips from the Assignments page out to individual product reports, which fits teachers finding the students they needed without leaving.
                    p My takeaway: an opinionated default earns its place when it matches how people already rank their work, and it goes wrong fast when it doesn’t. That’s why we tested before we built, and why every group still opens and closes.

            +folioFooter(true, 'We had to get student names through the assignment model and the API before any layout could show them.', {href: '/green-loom.html', label: 'Next case: Green Loom'})

        script(src="assets/js/assignment-demo.js" defer)
